From c3f5e004e56534a333702620f60907e87a6f8dae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:57:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][Windowing] The content type of the AVI InfoFrame:
 Cinema for videos, Game for games

Every driver told the display what it always tells it, a film or the menus
alike: NVIDIA and amdgpu "IT content, graphics" (ITC 1, CN 0), i915 and xe
"no data". Kodi never set DRM's "content type" connector property. A TV that
chooses its picture mode by the content type did not know that a film is a
film: an LG TV with Filmmaker Mode Auto Start goes to Filmmaker Mode on
Cinema, as a Kaleidescape player makes it do.

"Content type" (System > Display, videoscreen.hadescontenttype, Expert; shown
where the connector has the property), decided in windowing/gbm/ContentType.h,
which a host test runs:
- Automatic, the default: a video is said to be Cinema and a game of
  RetroPlayer Game, each only where the display declares that content type in
  the CNC bits of its HDMI VSDB (the LG C5 declares Cinema and Game; nothing is
  said to a display that does not); the menus "No Data", each driver's own
  value, as LibreELEC sends it; native Dolby Vision that the TV processes
  (Standard, the HDMI 1.4b signalling) "No Data", which every driver of Hades
  sends as ITC 0, as a licensed Dolby Vision set-top box does; Low-Latency and
  HDR over the tunnel are videos.
- As LibreELEC: Kodi does not set it; a value that Kodi itself left goes back
  to "No Data", one that another program set stays.

The AVI InfoFrame changes only with a mode set in i915 (mode_changed, and a
fastset does not take a new AVI InfoFrame), in amdgpu (mode_changed; a stream
that comes out the same keeps the old one) and in NVIDIA's driver of Hades
(nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9085, nvidia-legacy-open-9082). So the content
type is staged only in a commit that allows a mode set and changes the timing
of the output anyway (the refresh rate or size of a video, the mode of the
menus after it), and in the transport of native Dolby Vision and its end,
which are mode sets in every driver: never with a mode set of its own, never
one black screen more. A video that plays in the mode of the menus keeps their
value, and kodi.log says so once. A commit that changes the property allows a
mode set (CDRMAtomic, as for "Broadcast RGB"), as the guard for any other
request. The transport names it as a setting (PartOf()): its end gives back
what the menus are to have by the setting then (HandBackIntents()).

kodi.log says what the display declares and the setting when an output is
read, one line for each change of the value with what the AVI InfoFrame says,
and when a value waits for a change of mode. Not a byte of the picture changes.
